{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-47367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-47367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f43eaae6ef55f259a53e30dc6c68dacc87af721430f12caee8bc805a47cddce"} {"question" "q-47367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-47367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-47367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/assets/ec8101640f2bd84506d1c47b92f088675080d87eafff4000a4f3e0d14ccebb55.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-2$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ shows a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f43eaae6ef55f259a53e30dc6c68dacc87af721430f12caee8bc805a47cddce"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f43eaae6ef55f259a53e30dc6c68dacc87af721430f12caee8bc805a47cddce"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>-1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1$, and hence it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f43eaae6ef55f259a53e30dc6c68dacc87af721430f12caee8bc805a47cddce"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}"] ["math" "g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}"] ["math" "h(x)=2|x-1|"] ["math" "p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}"] ["math" "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<1 \\\\ x+2,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-73264" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f43eaae6ef55f259a53e30dc6c68dacc87af721430f12caee8bc805a47cddce"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"} {"question" "q-101997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/state.json" "file_sha256" "fb941013ac10640db646f7e00fd38e7f9df3dd1ab907742a472a4f596dfbb9b0"}] "usage" {"q-47367" []} "basis" 1070 "retractions" [[17592186115793 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n$f(x) = {x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$\n\n1. It is continuous and differentiable at $x = 1$\n2. It is **not** continuous at $x = 1$ but it is differentiable at $x = 1$\n3. It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x ∈ (-2, 2)$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186114130 17592186114131 17592186114132 17592186114133 17592186114134] "immutable_fields" [[17592186114129 :answer-field/correct] [17592186114129 :answer-field/id] [17592186114129 :answer-field/key] [17592186114129 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
